abstract: >-
  Version 0.3.4 corrects the interval of the difference that MAG-S3 reports for H3: the frozen analysis multiplies
  the exact conditional interval by the observed share of discordant birds, which made it too narrow, 0.369-0.440
  instead of 0.34-0.54; the verdict rests on the exact McNemar test and stays not met. It adds note 10 to the notes
  after release of MAG-S3c: computed directly between the dephasing levels of the run, the half-rate of the
  projection of the odd dipole is 3.2-5.9e7 per second in the real pairs, 1-7% below the values on the levels of the
  run and inside the range estimated from experiment, except one axis of W_D above 1e8. The notes now state that the
  gain from axial disorder holds when the neurons keep their modulation depth and that the bound of a null result
  belongs to an estimate that knows the bird's retina. The frozen files, the runs and the verdicts are unchanged.
  Version 0.3.3 adds note 9 to the notes after release of MAG-S3c. The recombination rate of the Cry4a set,
  3.4e5 per second, is the estimate of Xu et al. (2021) for the FAD-W_D pair, and the protocol applied it to both
  pairs. With the rate estimated for FAD-W_C, about 1.2e7 per second, every CISS form computed at the angle of
  Kobori et al. (2026) lies above the threshold of the dipole test in the W_C pair, theirs 35-48 times, and at
  axial order 0.4 the coherence form exceeds the approximate sphere-detection benchmark of MAG-S3. The frozen
  files, the run and the verdicts are those of version 0.3.0.
  Version 0.3.2 makes four wording fixes to the notes after release of MAG-S3c: when an oriented sample reads the
  whole odd dipole, what the check Q1 covers, bounds rounded up and the relaxation levels of the cells; the numbers,
  the frozen files, the run and the verdicts are those of version 0.3.1.
  Version 0.3.1 adds notes after release to MAG-S3c. With the definition of Kobori et al. (2026), CISS
  efficiency 100(1 - cos chi), the form of their model (birth at chi, recombination at -chi) gives an odd part below
  the threshold of the dipole test along the pair axis at the Cry4a rates of the run by the stricter transfer, and
  far above it at fast recombination without relaxation; the coherence form and the other polarisation forms stay visible. This
  withdraws a statement of version 0.3.0 that the W_C pair stands at the threshold at their scale. The exchange
  coupling they fitted, 22 uT, changes their form by 5-17% and lowers the response of the polarisation form P-same
  in the W_C pair by a factor of 1.7-2.6. The half-rate of 3.3-6.2e7 per second belongs to the projection of
  the odd dipole on the receptor axis; its full length, which dephasing also turns, halves at 4.5-6.7e7 per
  second or above 1e8 in the W_C pair and above 1e8 in the W_D pair. The frozen files, the run and the verdicts
  are those of version 0.3.0.
  Version 0.3.0 adds MAG-S3c, a pre-registered simulation study of whether the conclusions of MAG-S3b survive the
  real geometry of the radical pair in cryptochrome 4 and spin relaxation. It recomputes the three-nucleus pair in
  the FAD–W_C and FAD–W_D pairs of pigeon cryptochrome 4 (PDB 6PU0), with their dipolar coupling, with singlet-triplet
  dephasing up to 1e8 and random fields up to 3e7 per second. Its protocol, code and notebook were frozen and their
  SHA-256 posted in Telegram no later than the minute the run started; the run was continued in parts of 1.5 h after a flaw of the pause, a deviation
  recorded before the continuation. All eleven invariants held and a reanalysis reproduced every value. The odd
  dipole follows the hyperfine tensors, the singlet-triplet coherence creates it, its projection on the receptor axis falls by half at a dephasing
  rate of 3.3–6.2e7 per second (above 1e8 for one axis), inside the range estimated from experiment for Cry4a or above it, and the dipole test sees both candidate pairs.
  The dominance of the coherence form of CISS over the polarisation forms holds only without the dipolar coupling.
  Version 0.2.0 added MAG-S3b to MAG-S3. MAG-S3b is a pre-registered simulation study of whether the odd part of a
  radical-pair (cryptochrome 4) compass code can carry the sign of the geomagnetic field to neurons through
  chirality-induced spin selectivity (CISS), how the polar and axial order of cryptochromes gates it, and whether a
  blind estimate recovers its size from a MAG-S3 recording. Its protocol, code and notebook were frozen and their
  SHA-256 posted before the run was read; the run started three minutes before the freeze on the same files, a
  deviation recorded with the release. All invariants held. The coherence form of CISS gave 253 times more odd part
  than the polarisation forms, the blind estimate was calibrated, and a dipole test found the odd part at an
  effective admixture five times below the antipodal test of MAG-S3. The odd part stayed below the antipodal
  threshold in the reference design, departed from proportionality at the largest CISS angle, and did not follow
  the cos beta law, because the hyperfine tensors set the direction of the odd dipole together with the CISS axis.
  MAG-S3 itself, unchanged since version 0.1.1, is a simulation study of the recording experiment that could test
  whether neurons reading a radical-pair compass carry a population code shaped like the real projective plane RP2.
  Version 0.2.1 adds notes after release to MAG-S3b: exact consequences of its reciprocity criterion, one of which
  limits the reading of the odd part as a trace of CISS to pairs that recombine spin-selectively where they were born,
  and works found by a source check; the frozen files, the runs and the verdicts are those of version 0.2.0.
  Code is released under MIT; text, the protocols and the results under CC BY 4.0.
